The two patches provided are code clones. The similarity score between the two can be calculated by comparing the lines of code that have been changed in both patches. In this case, we will use a simple ratio-based approach to calculate the similarity score: 

```python
def calculate_similarity(patch1, patch2):
    common_lines = set()
    
    for line in patch1.split('\n'):
        if '+' not in line and '-' not in line:
            common_lines.add(line)
            
    for line in patch2.split('\n'):
        if '+' not in line and '-' not in line:
            common_lines.add(line)
    
    similarity = len(common_lines) / max((len(patch1.split('\n')), len(patch2.split('\n'))))
    return similarity
```

This function works by first identifying the lines that are unchanged in both patches (i.e., they do not start with '+' or '-'). It then calculates a ratio of these common lines to the total number of lines in each patch, giving us a score between 0 and 1. This can be interpreted as a measure of similarity between the two code snippets.

However, this approach does not take into account changes made by the developers. For example, if one developer adds an unnecessary comment to a line of code while another developer removes it, these lines would still count towards the common lines set and thus contribute to the similarity score. To address this issue, we could modify our function to only consider unchanged lines that are not solely whitespace:

```python
def calculate_similarity(patch1, patch2):
    common_lines = set()
    
    for line in patch1.split('\n'):
        if '+' not in line and '-' not in line and not line.isspace():
            common_lines.add(line)
            
    for line in patch2.split('\n'):
        if '+' not in line and '-' not in line and not line.isspace():
            common_lines.add(line)
    
    similarity = len(common_lines) / max((len(patch1.split('\n')), len(patch2.split('\n'))))
    return similarity
```

This approach should provide a more accurate measure of the similarity between the two code snippets, taking into account only the actual changes made by the developers and not any extraneous changes. 

