Cosine similarity measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors projected in a multi-dimensional space. It's a measure of orientation and not magnitude, so it can be more suitable for our task than other metrics like Jaccard index or Levenshtein distance. 

To calculate cosine similarity, we first need to convert each patch into a vector representation (a list of word frequencies in this case). Then, we can use the formula:

cosine_similarity = A·B / ||A|| ||B||

where A and B are vectors representing two patches, "||A||" and "||B||" are magnitudes of vectors A and B respectively. 

In our case, each patch can be represented as a vector where the i-th element is the count of occurrences of the i-th word in the patch. We then calculate cosine similarity between these two vectors to assess their similarity.

The resulting score will range from -1 (exactly opposite) to 1 (identical), with 0 indicating orthogonality, and values in between indicating varying degrees of similarity. A higher score indicates a greater degree of similarity.

