One possible solution is to convert the source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) and then compare the ASTs of the two patches. The AST represents the syntactic structure of a program in a tree-like format. It can be used as a more accurate measure of similarity than token-based text comparison because it takes into account not only the tokens but also their order, nesting level, and relationships between them.

To convert source code to an AST, we can use tools like JDT (Java Development Tools) in Eclipse or similar libraries for other programming languages. Once we have the ASTs, we can compare them using various tree edit distance algorithms such as LCS (Longest Common Subsequence), Edit Distance, etc.

Here is a simple example of how to use JDT to generate an AST:

```java
import org.eclipse.jdt.core.dom.*;
...
JavaCore javaCore = new JavaParser().getJavaFileManager(null, null);
