After generating the ASTs, we can compare them using tree edit distance algorithms like LCS or Edit Distance:

```java
public int calculateEditDistance(String s1, String s2) {
    int len1 = s1.length();
    int len2 = s2.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[len1 + 1][len2 + 1];
    
    for (int i = 0; i <= len1; i++) {
        dp[i][0] = i;
    }
    
    for (int j = 0; j <= len2; j++) {
        dp[0][j] = j;
    }
    
    for (int i = 1; i <= len1; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= len2; j++) {
            if (s1.charAt(i - 1) == s2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i - 1][j] + 1, Math.min(dp[i][j - 1] + 1, dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1));
            }
        }
    }
    
    return dp[len1][len2];
}
```

This function calculates the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions, deletions or substitutions) needed to change one string into the other. The similarity score can then be calculated as 1 minus the edit distance divided by the maximum possible edit distance (which is the length of the longer string).

Please note that this approach requires a deep understanding of both source code and tree-edit distance algorithms, and it may not work perfectly for all cases. But it's a good starting point for more advanced analysis.

