### Response
To use your custom control in XAML, you need to declare the namespace for your local assembly and then use the fully qualified name of your class. 

Firstly, add a reference to your project in the XAML file:
```xaml
xmlns:local="clr-namespace:YourNamespace;assembly=YourAssemblyName"
```
Replace "YourNamespace" with the namespace where your custom control is defined and replace "YourAssemblyName" with the name of your assembly. 

Then, you can use it in XAML like this:
```xaml
<local:CustomEntry PlaceholderColor="Red"/>
```
This will create an instance of `CustomEntry` with its `PlaceholderColor` property set to Red. Make sure that the namespace and assembly name match your project's configuration. 

Also, ensure you have correctly registered your custom control in Xamarin.Forms:
```csharp
public class CustomEntry : Entry
{
    // ...
}

[assembly: ExportRenderer(typeof(CustomEntry), typeof(CustomEntryRenderer))]
namespace YourNamespace.Droid
{
    public class CustomEntryRenderer : EntryRenderer
    {
        protected override void OnElementChanged(ElementChangedEventArgs<Entry> e)
        {
            base.OnElementChanged(e);
            
            if (Control != null && e.NewElement is CustomEntry customEntry)
            {
                Control.SetTextColor(customEntry.PlaceholderColor.ToAndroid());
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, `CustomEntryRenderer` class should be in a platform-specific project (like Android or iOS). The renderer is responsible for applying the custom properties to the native control. In this case, it sets the text color of the entry based on the `PlaceholderColor` property. 

